98],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c}$ be three vectors such that\n\\[\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Compute $\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})\\), we will use the distributive property of the cross product, which states that for any vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}\\), \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{w}\\), and any scalars \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), we have \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times (m\\mathbf{v} + n\\mathbf{w}) = m(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + n(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w})\\). Applying this property, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = 3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), so \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). We are also given \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Substituting these into the expression, we get:\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2b4a6b459c29e20feb7a9b1376aea9664375c033bb8d05d42076bd8ceea876ce"},"seconds":54.242873430252075,"seed":792152318969961804,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})\\), we use the distributive property of the cross product. According to this property, for any vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}\\), \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{w}\\), and any scalars \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), we have \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times (m\\mathbf{v} + n\\mathbf{w}) = m(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + n(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w})\\). Applying this property, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = 3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nWe know from the given information that \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). By the property of the cross product, \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nWe also know that \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}\\). By the property of the cross product, \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSubstituting these into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = 3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we compute the scalar multiplications and then the vector subtraction:\n\n\\[\n3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 + 2 \\\\ -21 - 14 \\\\ -6 + 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})\\), we use the distributive property of the cross product. According to this property, for any vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}\\), \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{w}\\), and any scalars \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), we have \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times (m\\mathbf{v} + n\\mathbf{w}) = m(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + n(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w})\\). Applying this property, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = 3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nWe know from the given information that \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). By the property of the cross product, \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nWe also know that \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}\\). By the property of the cross product, \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSubstituting these into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = 3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we compute the scalar multiplications and then the vector subtraction:\n\n\\[\n3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 + 2 \\\\ -21 - 14 \\\\ -6 + 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]","id":"precalculus:429"}
